1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:01,070
You wake up,

2
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:02,790
and the glowing numbers are gone.

3
00:00:03,310 --> 00:00:04,590
Your head feels quiet.

4
00:00:05,090 --> 00:00:06,450
Normal, even.

5
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:08,130
Until you step out of your bedroom,

6
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:09,530
close the front door,

7
00:00:09,850 --> 00:00:11,570
and take two steps down the hallway.

8
00:00:12,670 --> 00:00:13,210
A voice.

9
00:00:13,990 --> 00:00:16,310
Faint, clear, and right in your ear.

10
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:18,770
Your roommate, talking inside

11
00:00:18,770 --> 00:00:20,030
the apartment you just left.

12
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:21,090
You freeze.

13
00:00:21,810 --> 00:00:23,110
You didn't leave your phone on.

14
00:00:23,230 --> 00:00:24,310
You don't have an earpiece in.

15
00:00:24,950 --> 00:00:26,370
For the next seven days,

16
00:00:26,490 --> 00:00:27,470
whenever you leave a room,

17
00:00:27,470 --> 00:00:30,350
you can hear every single sentence spoken about you.

18
00:00:30,630 --> 00:00:31,330
Your friends.

19
00:00:31,610 --> 00:00:32,110
Your coworkers.

20
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:33,230
Your family.

21
00:00:33,770 --> 00:00:35,370
The stranger you bumped into on the subway.

22
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:36,970
And by day seven,

23
00:00:37,190 --> 00:00:39,330
the quietest whisper in the city will change

24
00:00:39,330 --> 00:00:41,370
how you treat every person in your life.

25
00:00:42,070 --> 00:00:45,130
At first, your heart pounds through your ribcage.

26
00:00:45,130 --> 00:00:46,230
Because your entire life,

27
00:00:46,230 --> 00:00:47,650
you've wondered: what do people

28
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:49,430
actually say when the door closes?

29
00:00:50,090 --> 00:00:51,470
You step outside the coffee shop.

30
00:00:51,850 --> 00:00:52,310
You listen.

31
00:00:52,310 --> 00:00:53,130
Wait.

32
00:00:53,970 --> 00:00:55,030
They're not mocking you.

33
00:00:55,390 --> 00:00:56,910
In fact, after twenty seconds,

34
00:00:56,910 --> 00:00:58,770
they stop talking about you completely.

35
00:00:59,350 --> 00:01:00,990
They're arguing about parking tickets.

36
00:01:01,730 --> 00:01:02,890
Lesson one, before breakfast:

37
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:05,950
people talk about you far less than you think.

38
00:01:06,710 --> 00:01:08,910
Psychologists call this the spotlight effect.

39
00:01:09,350 --> 00:01:11,050
We live inside our own heads all day,

40
00:01:11,350 --> 00:01:13,050
so we naturally assume everyone

41
00:01:13,050 --> 00:01:14,570
else is thinking about us too.

42
00:01:15,070 --> 00:01:16,570
In famous experiments at Cornell,

43
00:01:17,050 --> 00:01:19,190
researchers found people overestimate how much

44
00:01:19,190 --> 00:01:21,010
others notice them by more than double.

45
00:01:21,010 --> 00:01:22,270
Most of the time,

46
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:23,850
the moment you leave the room,

47
00:01:23,950 --> 00:01:25,270
you vanish from their minds.

48
00:01:25,710 --> 00:01:26,790
And that's not insulting.

49
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:27,850
It's a relief.

50
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:29,710
Then day two arrives,

51
00:01:29,710 --> 00:01:30,890
and you walk into work.

52
00:01:31,390 --> 00:01:33,470
At lunch, you stand up from the break room,

53
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:35,130
excuse yourself to wash your hands,

54
00:01:35,130 --> 00:01:36,570
and linger outside the door.

55
00:01:37,290 --> 00:01:38,150
Your jaw drops.

56
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:39,250
Kevin.

57
00:01:39,770 --> 00:01:41,150
The guy who barely says five

58
00:01:41,150 --> 00:01:42,270
words in morning meetings.

59
00:01:42,630 --> 00:01:44,410
He isn't taking credit for your work.

60
00:01:44,570 --> 00:01:45,970
He's protecting you.

61
00:01:46,570 --> 00:01:47,850
Researchers who study workplace

62
00:01:47,850 --> 00:01:49,730
behaviour found something fascinating:

63
00:01:50,170 --> 00:01:52,930
We assume workplace gossip is always malicious,

64
00:01:53,330 --> 00:01:55,590
people tearing each other down behind closed doors.

65
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:58,910
But studies by organizational sociologists

66
00:01:58,910 --> 00:02:00,950
found that over seventy percent of

67
00:02:00,950 --> 00:02:02,970
everyday office chatter is neutral,

68
00:02:03,410 --> 00:02:05,050
and a huge chunk is what scientists

69
00:02:05,050 --> 00:02:07,030
call prosocial gossip.

70
00:02:07,490 --> 00:02:09,130
People sharing information to protect

71
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:10,830
coworkers and enforce fairness.

72
00:02:11,550 --> 00:02:13,690
Kevin wasn't gossiping to hurt anyone.

73
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:15,330
He was watching your back.

74
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:17,010
On day three,

75
00:02:17,150 --> 00:02:18,450
you hear the dark side.

76
00:02:18,950 --> 00:02:20,030
You drop your pen in the corridor,

77
00:02:20,490 --> 00:02:21,290
step around the corner,

78
00:02:21,550 --> 00:02:22,030
and listen.

79
00:02:22,770 --> 00:02:23,890
One coworker tells another:

80
00:02:24,590 --> 00:02:26,390
I heard Magi was ten minutes late yesterday

81
00:02:26,390 --> 00:02:27,910
because he got stopped by security.

82
00:02:28,870 --> 00:02:29,870
Ten minutes later,

83
00:02:29,950 --> 00:02:31,650
you hear that same coworker talking

84
00:02:31,650 --> 00:02:33,090
to someone on the fourth floor:

85
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:36,170
Magi had an incident with building security yesterday.

86
00:02:36,410 --> 00:02:37,850
Something about his pass being revoked.

87
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:39,770
By three in the afternoon,

88
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:42,750
the whisper reaching your ear is completely unhinged:

89
00:02:42,750 --> 00:02:45,010
Did you hear Magi got escorted out by guards?

90
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:47,530
You stand by the elevators,

91
00:02:47,930 --> 00:02:48,270
stunned.

92
00:02:49,050 --> 00:02:49,810
You weren't late.

93
00:02:50,330 --> 00:02:51,730
You stopped to tie your shoelace.

94
00:02:52,370 --> 00:02:54,990
How did tying a shoelace turn into armed guards?

95
00:02:56,310 --> 00:02:59,450
In 1932, psychologist Frederic Bartlett ran experiments

96
00:02:59,450 --> 00:03:01,790
on how stories travel from person to person.

97
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:04,050
He found that rumors mutate through

98
00:03:04,050 --> 00:03:05,390
three predictable stages:

99
00:03:06,050 --> 00:03:08,290
Leveling, people drop the boring details.

100
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:11,750
Sharpening, people exaggerate the dramatic parts.

101
00:03:12,150 --> 00:03:14,330
And assimilation, people twist the story

102
00:03:14,330 --> 00:03:16,110
to fit what they already believe.

103
00:03:17,030 --> 00:03:19,090
Every time a sentence travels behind your back,

104
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:21,190
it loses truth and gains drama.

105
00:03:21,890 --> 00:03:23,390
The whisper wasn't about who you are.

106
00:03:23,730 --> 00:03:25,090
It was about human entertainment.

107
00:03:26,110 --> 00:03:26,810
On day four,

108
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:28,490
you hear the sentence you were dreading.

109
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:30,070
You walk out to the car park.

110
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:31,310
Your ears buzz.

111
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:32,990
The whisper clicks on.

112
00:03:33,450 --> 00:03:35,410
It hits like ice water down your neck.

113
00:03:35,410 --> 00:03:37,410
Your hands grip the steering wheel.

114
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:38,710
Your stomach knots.

115
00:03:39,890 --> 00:03:40,450
Exhausting?

116
00:03:41,010 --> 00:03:41,850
Overthinks everything?

117
00:03:42,910 --> 00:03:44,950
You've known this person for seven years.

118
00:03:45,390 --> 00:03:46,570
You helped them move apartments.

119
00:03:47,170 --> 00:03:48,430
You listened to their breakups.

120
00:03:49,250 --> 00:03:50,210
For three hours,

121
00:03:50,350 --> 00:03:51,230
you sit in your car,

122
00:03:51,510 --> 00:03:53,410
replaying those eight words on repeat.

123
00:03:54,270 --> 00:03:56,410
Psychologists call this the negativity bias.

124
00:03:57,030 --> 00:03:59,150
Our ancestors survived by paying ten times

125
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:01,090
more attention to danger than to safety.

126
00:04:01,610 --> 00:04:03,390
A predator in the bushes matters more

127
00:04:03,390 --> 00:04:04,850
than a ripe berry on a branch.

128
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:07,690
So your brain treats one piece of negative

129
00:04:07,690 --> 00:04:09,430
gossip like a life-or-death wound,

130
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:12,290
while completely erasing a hundred kind things.

131
00:04:12,910 --> 00:04:14,390
Then you realize something painful.

132
00:04:15,570 --> 00:04:16,810
Are you exhausting sometimes?

133
00:04:17,390 --> 00:04:18,090
Do you overthink?

134
00:04:18,930 --> 00:04:19,550
Yes.

135
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:20,630
You do.

136
00:04:20,630 --> 00:04:23,710
A cruel sentence spoken behind your back hurts.

137
00:04:24,470 --> 00:04:26,690
But sometimes, buried underneath the sting,

138
00:04:27,150 --> 00:04:29,490
it's the only mirror honest enough to tell

139
00:04:29,490 --> 00:04:31,210
you what no one will say to your face.

140
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:32,790
By day five,

141
00:04:32,930 --> 00:04:34,890
the whispers are driving you crazy.

142
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:36,590
Every time you leave a room,

143
00:04:36,690 --> 00:04:37,750
someone says something.

144
00:04:38,070 --> 00:04:39,870
A jogger commenting on your pace.

145
00:04:40,130 --> 00:04:42,130
A cashier remarking on your change.

146
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:43,890
Why do humans do this?

147
00:04:44,210 --> 00:04:45,230
Why can't anyone just keep

148
00:04:45,230 --> 00:04:46,630
their thoughts to themselves?

149
00:04:47,470 --> 00:04:48,310
It turns out,

150
00:04:48,870 --> 00:04:50,670
evolutionary anthropologists studied this.

151
00:04:51,090 --> 00:04:53,770
Robin Dunbar, the famous Oxford anthropologist,

152
00:04:54,010 --> 00:04:56,450
discovered that roughly sixty-five percent of all

153
00:04:56,450 --> 00:04:59,590
human conversation across cultures is gossip:

154
00:04:59,590 --> 00:05:01,490
talking about who did what,

155
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:02,530
who is trustworthy,

156
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:03,950
and who broke a rule.

157
00:05:04,530 --> 00:05:06,870
In primates, bonding is done by physical grooming,

158
00:05:07,270 --> 00:05:09,350
picking bugs out of fur for hours.

159
00:05:09,870 --> 00:05:12,130
Humans formed tribes too large for grooming.

160
00:05:12,390 --> 00:05:13,530
So we evolved language,

161
00:05:13,970 --> 00:05:15,930
and language became social grooming.

162
00:05:16,550 --> 00:05:18,930
Gossip isn't a modern flaw of bored people.

163
00:05:19,290 --> 00:05:21,050
It was the original social glue

164
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:23,190
that kept ancient tribes alive.

165
00:05:23,530 --> 00:05:25,430
Without it, no one would know who to trust.

166
00:05:25,830 --> 00:05:28,230
The problem isn't that people talk behind your back.

167
00:05:28,230 --> 00:05:29,830
The problem is that you were never

168
00:05:29,830 --> 00:05:32,670
biologically equipped to hear it.

169
00:05:32,670 --> 00:05:33,370
On day six,

170
00:05:33,410 --> 00:05:35,510
you hear the whisper that changes everything.

171
00:05:36,070 --> 00:05:37,790
You step into the hallway to make a phone call.

172
00:05:38,150 --> 00:05:39,710
A sharp voice cuts through your ears.

173
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:43,250
Honestly, that Magi guy seems in over his head.

174
00:05:43,590 --> 00:05:45,350
We should ask for someone else on this account.

175
00:05:45,930 --> 00:05:46,710
Your heart sinks.

176
00:05:47,090 --> 00:05:48,230
Your chest tightens.

177
00:05:48,610 --> 00:05:49,670
You reach for the door handle,

178
00:05:49,670 --> 00:05:52,210
ready to walk in and apologize or defend yourself.

179
00:05:52,610 --> 00:05:54,070
And then another voice speaks.

180
00:05:54,910 --> 00:05:55,450
That's not fair.

181
00:05:55,450 --> 00:05:57,790
Magi spent three weeks fixing the

182
00:05:57,790 --> 00:05:59,430
data errors your team missed.

183
00:05:59,590 --> 00:06:01,010
If there's an issue with this account,

184
00:06:01,210 --> 00:06:02,050
it's on my desk,

185
00:06:02,170 --> 00:06:02,750
not his.

186
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:03,970
You stop.

187
00:06:04,570 --> 00:06:05,410
It's your boss.

188
00:06:05,790 --> 00:06:07,170
The one who never gives compliments.

189
00:06:07,490 --> 00:06:09,050
The one who sends one-word emails

190
00:06:09,050 --> 00:06:10,510
and looks bored in presentations.

191
00:06:11,310 --> 00:06:12,690
He didn't know you could hear him.

192
00:06:12,930 --> 00:06:13,910
There was no audience.

193
00:06:14,030 --> 00:06:14,730
No cameras.

194
00:06:15,030 --> 00:06:16,810
No karma score floating above his head.

195
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:19,330
He defended your name when you weren't

196
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:20,630
in the room to defend yourself.

197
00:06:21,130 --> 00:06:24,370
There is no praise on earth sweeter than the

198
00:06:24,370 --> 00:06:25,930
praise you were never supposed to hear.

199
00:06:26,330 --> 00:06:27,910
And you realize something profound:

200
00:06:28,550 --> 00:06:30,830
You've spent your entire life worrying about

201
00:06:30,830 --> 00:06:32,910
the people who talk about you when you leave.

202
00:06:33,550 --> 00:06:36,470
When the only people who actually matter are the ones

203
00:06:36,470 --> 00:06:38,570
who don't let anyone tear you down while you're gone.

204
00:06:39,130 --> 00:06:39,970
On day seven,

205
00:06:39,970 --> 00:06:41,750
you decide to test something.

206
00:06:42,110 --> 00:06:44,470
All day, you deliberately don't listen.

207
00:06:45,010 --> 00:06:45,990
When you step out of a room,

208
00:06:45,990 --> 00:06:47,230
you don't wait for the click.

209
00:06:47,230 --> 00:06:48,490
You don't strain your ears.

210
00:06:49,170 --> 00:06:49,870
You walk forward.

211
00:06:50,410 --> 00:06:51,630
You focus on what you're doing,

212
00:06:51,890 --> 00:06:52,390
who you're helping,

213
00:06:52,670 --> 00:06:54,510
and the person standing right in front of you.

214
00:06:55,150 --> 00:06:57,410
And a strange peace settles in.

215
00:06:57,710 --> 00:06:59,930
Because you finally understand the secret.

216
00:07:00,710 --> 00:07:02,290
What people say about you behind your

217
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:04,830
back is almost never about you.

218
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:06,730
It's about their own insecurity.

219
00:07:07,210 --> 00:07:07,970
Their own boredom.

220
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:10,150
Their need to bond with whoever

221
00:07:10,150 --> 00:07:11,450
is standing next to them.

222
00:07:12,070 --> 00:07:13,730
When someone speaks ill of you,

223
00:07:13,730 --> 00:07:15,470
they aren't describing your character.

224
00:07:16,030 --> 00:07:16,950
They're revealing their own.

225
00:07:17,530 --> 00:07:20,390
And when someone speaks well of you in an empty room,

226
00:07:20,830 --> 00:07:21,890
that's not reputation.

227
00:07:22,670 --> 00:07:23,270
That's love.

228
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:25,550
At 11:59 p.m.,

229
00:07:25,730 --> 00:07:27,030
the clock approaches midnight.

230
00:07:27,750 --> 00:07:28,830
The whispers grow soft.

231
00:07:29,370 --> 00:07:29,890
Then faint.

232
00:07:30,410 --> 00:07:31,810
Then completely silent.

233
00:07:32,890 --> 00:07:33,930
The superpower is gone.

234
00:07:34,650 --> 00:07:36,050
You stand in the quiet room,

235
00:07:36,050 --> 00:07:37,450
looking at your reflection.

236
00:07:38,090 --> 00:07:39,430
No numbers above your head.

237
00:07:39,630 --> 00:07:41,050
No voices in your ears.

238
00:07:41,510 --> 00:07:42,650
Just you.

239
00:07:42,650 --> 00:07:45,250
Here is what seven days of hearing

240
00:07:45,250 --> 00:07:46,590
every whisper taught you:

241
00:07:47,110 --> 00:07:48,770
Most people aren't talking about you at all.

242
00:07:49,470 --> 00:07:52,190
Rumors are just stories told by bored minds.

243
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:56,350
Criticism can hurt and still have a seed of truth.

244
00:07:56,850 --> 00:07:58,450
And the truest measure of friendship

245
00:07:58,450 --> 00:08:00,570
isn't who smiles in your face,

246
00:08:01,090 --> 00:08:03,350
it's who defends your name when the door closes.

247
00:08:04,450 --> 00:08:05,230
So tomorrow morning,

248
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:06,830
when you walk into a room and

249
00:08:06,830 --> 00:08:08,230
someone leaves to grab coffee,

250
00:08:08,770 --> 00:08:10,330
remember what it felt like to be

251
00:08:10,330 --> 00:08:11,590
on the other side of the door.

252
00:08:12,730 --> 00:08:14,590
Tomorrow, the silence continues.

253
00:08:15,230 --> 00:08:16,730
Until you look at someone's face,

254
00:08:17,090 --> 00:08:18,990
and you see a tiny digital clock

255
00:08:18,990 --> 00:08:20,590
ticking down on their forehead.

256
00:08:21,310 --> 00:08:23,370
Years, days, hours,

257
00:08:24,150 --> 00:08:24,370
seconds.

258
00:08:25,250 --> 00:08:27,410
What if you could see everyone's lifespan?

259
00:08:28,190 --> 00:08:29,170
If you want to know what happens

260
00:08:29,170 --> 00:08:30,610
when the clock hits zero,

261
00:08:31,210 --> 00:08:31,690
subscribe.

262
00:08:31,690 --> 00:08:34,610
Because tomorrow, the countdown begins.
